WEBVTT

NOTE Internet Monitor tour: settings (en). Generated by tools/tutorial/build.cjs.

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.167
Welcome to Internet Monitor!

2
00:00:02.167 --> 00:00:07.500
This short tour explains every part of
the Settings page, one section at a time.

3
00:00:07.900 --> 00:00:12.700
The list on the left shows all the sections.
Click a name to jump straight to it.

4
00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:16.967
Monitoring decides how closely
the app watches your connection.

5
00:00:17.367 --> 00:00:22.492
Check every sets the seconds between checks.
A smaller number spots a drop sooner.

6
00:00:22.492 --> 00:00:27.367
A bigger number puts less load on your network.
Five seconds suits most homes.

7
00:00:27.767 --> 00:00:30.677
Failures before disconnected
is how many checks in

8
00:00:30.677 --> 00:00:33.588
a row must fail before the
app calls it an outage.

9
00:00:33.588 --> 00:00:35.567
Raise it if tiny blips bother you.

10
00:00:35.967 --> 00:00:41.451
Packet loss means messages that never arrive.
Latency is the delay, in milliseconds.

11
00:00:41.451 --> 00:00:44.976
Above these limits,
the connection is marked unstable.

12
00:00:44.976 --> 00:00:48.567
Raise them if your line is
often slow but still usable.

13
00:00:48.967 --> 00:00:51.852
Also ping the router checks
your own router too.

14
00:00:51.852 --> 00:00:56.180
That tells a problem in your home
apart from a problem at your provider.

15
00:00:56.180 --> 00:00:59.967
It may need admin rights,
meaning full control of the computer.

16
00:01:00.367 --> 00:01:04.522
Check targets are the places the
app contacts to test your internet.

17
00:01:04.522 --> 00:01:09.167
The defaults are large, reliable services,
so most people never change them.

18
00:01:09.567 --> 00:01:14.367
TCP targets are a computer's address plus
a port, which is like a door number.

19
00:01:14.367 --> 00:01:16.767
The app knocks and waits for an answer.

20
00:01:17.167 --> 00:01:21.734
HTTP targets are web pages that
must give a quick OK reply.

21
00:01:21.734 --> 00:01:26.223
DNS names are looked up in DNS,
the internet's phone book.

22
00:01:26.223 --> 00:01:31.100
If websites fail while everything
else works, DNS is often why.

23
00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:37.300
Press Test targets to try them all now.
Each one shows its reply time, or why it failed.

24
00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:40.684
Alerts send a message
when your internet drops.

25
00:01:40.684 --> 00:01:44.367
Test desktop alert shows a
sample pop-up on this computer.

26
00:01:44.767 --> 00:01:47.932
A webhook is a web address
that accepts messages.

27
00:01:47.932 --> 00:01:52.842
Paste one from Discord or Slack,
and every outage is posted to that channel.

28
00:01:52.842 --> 00:01:57.300
Home automation tools can use it too.
Send test checks that it works.

29
00:01:57.700 --> 00:02:01.501
For Telegram, paste your bot's token,
which works like its

30
00:02:01.501 --> 00:02:05.367
password, and the chat ID,
which says where to send alerts.

31
00:02:05.767 --> 00:02:08.044
ntfy is a free phone app for alerts.

32
00:02:08.044 --> 00:02:10.827
Install it, subscribe to a topic with a name

33
00:02:10.827 --> 00:02:13.927
that's hard to guess,
and paste its address here.

34
00:02:13.927 --> 00:02:16.900
The token is only needed
for a protected topic.

35
00:02:17.300 --> 00:02:21.230
Notifications decide what
reaches you, where, and when.

36
00:02:21.230 --> 00:02:24.375
Play a sound adds a sound to desktop alerts.

37
00:02:24.375 --> 00:02:28.233
Set the volume, then press
Try this volume to hear it.

38
00:02:28.633 --> 00:02:32.237
Quiet hours keep alerts calm
while you sleep or work.

39
00:02:32.237 --> 00:02:35.433
Turn them on, then set
when they start and end.

40
00:02:35.833 --> 00:02:37.478
Pick the days they apply.

41
00:02:37.478 --> 00:02:42.674
Then choose to mute the sound,
or to hold desktop alerts until quiet hours end.

42
00:02:42.674 --> 00:02:45.633
Webhook and Telegram
messages are still sent.

43
00:02:46.033 --> 00:02:51.449
What to send where is a grid. Each row is an event,
like Connection lost or Back online.

44
00:02:51.449 --> 00:02:55.633
Each column is a place to send it.
Switch on just the ones you want.

45
00:02:55.633 --> 00:02:58.833
A column stays off until
you set it up under Alerts.

46
00:02:59.233 --> 00:03:02.837
Every Monday at nine,
you also get a weekly summary.

47
00:03:02.837 --> 00:03:06.233
Press Send last 7 days now
to see one right away.

48
00:03:06.633 --> 00:03:11.537
Wait before alerting sets how many seconds
a drop must last before you're told.

49
00:03:11.537 --> 00:03:16.192
Shorter drops are still saved in your
history, they just don't disturb you.

50
00:03:16.192 --> 00:03:17.433
Zero alerts at once.

51
00:03:17.833 --> 00:03:22.979
Remind while down repeats the alert every
few minutes until the internet is back.

52
00:03:22.979 --> 00:03:24.567
Zero turns reminders off.

53
00:03:24.967 --> 00:03:27.359
This card is about an idea for later.

54
00:03:27.359 --> 00:03:31.691
Today, an alert can only leave your
home once the internet is back.

55
00:03:31.691 --> 00:03:35.893
An outside service could warn your
phone even during a power cut.

56
00:03:35.893 --> 00:03:38.221
If you want that, press I want this.

57
00:03:38.221 --> 00:03:42.100
It opens a page on GitHub,
and the app itself sends nothing.

58
00:03:42.500 --> 00:03:45.133
Alert sound lets you use your own sound.

59
00:03:45.133 --> 00:03:50.530
Upload an MP3 or WAV file, up to thirty
seconds long. Try the sound plays it here.

60
00:03:50.530 --> 00:03:54.151
Play like a real alert plays
it just as an alert would.

61
00:03:54.151 --> 00:03:58.100
After an upload, Use default
brings back the built-in sound.

62
00:03:58.500 --> 00:04:04.080
Speed test controls how your speed is measured.
Test length is how long each test runs.

63
00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:06.517
Longer is more accurate on fast lines.

64
00:04:06.517 --> 00:04:12.033
Parallel connections are downloads running
side by side, which helps fill a fast line.

65
00:04:12.433 --> 00:04:17.434
Alert below warns you when a test is
slower than this many megabits per second.

66
00:04:17.434 --> 00:04:20.599
Run automatically every
runs a test on a schedule,

67
00:04:20.599 --> 00:04:23.321
in minutes, so you collect proof over time.

68
00:04:23.321 --> 00:04:24.967
Zero turns either one off.

69
00:04:25.367 --> 00:04:28.430
This line estimates how
much data the tests use.

70
00:04:28.430 --> 00:04:31.367
That matters if your plan
has a monthly limit.

71
00:04:31.767 --> 00:04:34.894
Download and upload URLs
are the test servers.

72
00:04:34.894 --> 00:04:38.633
Leave the upload address empty
to skip the upload test.

73
00:04:39.033 --> 00:04:43.842
App holds general options.
Language changes the words on every screen.

74
00:04:43.842 --> 00:04:48.033
Theme switches between dark and light.
Both apply right away.

75
00:04:48.433 --> 00:04:54.052
Start with Windows opens the app when your
computer starts, so it never misses an outage.

76
00:04:54.052 --> 00:04:56.767
Where that isn't possible, it says so here.

77
00:04:57.167 --> 00:05:01.065
Dashboard port is the number at
the end of this page's address.

78
00:05:01.065 --> 00:05:05.830
Change it only if another program uses
the same number, then restart the app.

79
00:05:05.830 --> 00:05:08.367
Log folder is where your history is kept.

80
00:05:08.767 --> 00:05:13.757
Check for updates automatically asks
GitHub for a new version every six hours.

81
00:05:13.757 --> 00:05:16.700
You can turn it off and
check by hand instead.

82
00:05:17.100 --> 00:05:20.796
Every day, your logs are sealed,
so any change would show.

83
00:05:20.796 --> 00:05:25.256
Timestamp the sealed logs adds an
independent date stamp to each seal.

84
00:05:25.256 --> 00:05:29.844
It proves your records weren't edited later,
which helps in a complaint.

85
00:05:29.844 --> 00:05:32.967
Only a tiny fingerprint is sent,
never your data.

86
00:05:33.367 --> 00:05:36.890
Read the router's status asks
your router about the line,

87
00:05:36.890 --> 00:05:39.548
its speed and your data use, once a minute.

88
00:05:39.548 --> 00:05:43.567
It stays inside your home network,
and helps explain each outage.

89
00:05:43.967 --> 00:05:47.321
Add the router's password to
read deeper line details,

90
00:05:47.321 --> 00:05:50.427
like the noise margin,
which shows signal quality.

91
00:05:50.427 --> 00:05:53.595
The address and user name
usually work as they are.

92
00:05:53.595 --> 00:05:57.633
The password stays on this computer
and goes only to your router.

93
00:05:58.033 --> 00:06:00.558
Test the router password checks it once.

94
00:06:00.558 --> 00:06:05.167
If the router rejects it, the app stops
trying, so it can't lock you out.

95
00:06:05.567 --> 00:06:10.500
Updates shows your version.
Check for updates looks for a newer one right now.

96
00:06:10.900 --> 00:06:13.918
Open on your phone appears
only when the dashboard is

97
00:06:13.918 --> 00:06:17.164
open to your home network,
for example on a Raspberry Pi.

98
00:06:17.164 --> 00:06:20.979
Scan the code with your phone's
camera to open the dashboard there.

99
00:06:20.979 --> 00:06:24.567
Don't share a photo of it,
because it contains your access key.

100
00:06:24.967 --> 00:06:28.482
When you change a setting,
this bar appears at the bottom.

101
00:06:28.482 --> 00:06:32.300
Press Save changes to keep your edits,
or Discard to undo them.

102
00:06:32.300 --> 00:06:36.300
If you try to leave first,
the app asks before throwing them away.

103
00:06:36.700 --> 00:06:41.429
To watch this tour again, press How
this page works at the top of Settings,

104
00:06:41.429 --> 00:06:45.906
or open Video tours in the sidebar,
where every page has its own video.

105
00:06:45.906 --> 00:06:47.167
Thanks for watching!
